#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119600 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "41a8466d-8bc6-5811-a557-e5a4e840f51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4577" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Find $z.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 4x-2z &=1 \\\\ 3x+y-z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1x-1y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 4x+0y-2z &=1 \\\\ 3x+1y-1z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-(-2))+(-4+6)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =2+2+12 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-1)+(-8-3)-(4-0) \\\\  & =-1-11-4 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-16}{16}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e1eb00d6-77fe-5110-8095-a54e63981c09" :question/math-academy-id "e-4579" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Given that $x = 2,$ find the value of $k.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=-1 \\\\ kx+3y-2z &=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ k & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6-2)-2(2k+6)-(-k-9) \\\\  & =4-4k-12+k+9 \\\\  & =1-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x = \\dfrac{D_x}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(6-2)-2(2+6)-(-1-9) \\\\  & =-4-16+10 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D} = \\dfrac{-10}{1-3k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-10}{1-3k} & =2 \\\\ -10 & =2-6k \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119667 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6172" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-6z &=8 \\\\ -6x+4y+2z &=3 \\\\ 5x-5y-15z &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y+(-6)z &=8 \\\\ (-6)x+4y+2z &=3 \\\\ 5x+(-5)y+(-15)z &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n![](images/00/00a69fe1d48b41e376971f3669ae51668733d36dfda18c0f9cc736d5d071a441.png)\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n![](images/14/14e850c9af7c0b2fdd1e36299667b2cbe91c84e8bb90030b729ee23b296ad984.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178aa29c-c731-5575-b789-a031a7794737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269463} {:answer/id #uuid "32d6fe63-36e7-505d-b5ca-f8377d01d86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269733} {:answer/id #uuid "44193ff7-7933-5662-b23c-3e02bf36d32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269885} {:answer/id #uuid "9261b35a-1876-58e0-8b49-edd341555666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270549} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e86f2-180e-58a4-a5c7-b73059c1110a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44193ff7-7933-5662-b23c-3e02bf36d32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269885} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a02390-118a-5af6-b56f-a5456ae3d683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "254e5808-cdaa-58f1-bc6f-06ea580b1b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140043" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+5y-3z &=2 \\\\ 4x-5y+kz &=1 \\\\ -2x+10y-5z &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that  $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $160-70k,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|=160-70k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & k \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(25-10k)-5(-5+2k)-3(10-10) \\\\  & =50-20k+25-10k+0 \\\\  & =75-30k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(75-30k) & =160-70k \\\\ 150-60k & =160-70k \\\\ 10k & =10 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439acbed-ae94-5186-8b18-2a5683b17f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327522} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3d3044-4f96-5fc4-9219-3888cf7c6c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327615} {:answer/id #uuid "5d09d9f1-208c-5b8e-8e1d-8ec12e7f84ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327754} {:answer/id #uuid "786b3736-0f53-5024-9f0d-aeb608275a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327964} {:answer/id #uuid "da3ac648-6583-5128-9638-812bb3478223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "439acbed-ae94-5186-8b18-2a5683b17f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327522} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea644269-1c6a-5369-9cdc-49e7becab591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cf1a77a-780e-5431-8f91-cd363218bcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42623" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y-z &=1 \\\\ 2x-3z &=-2 \\\\ -x+y-2z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $42,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y+(-1)z &=1 \\\\ 2x+0y+(-3)z &=-2 \\\\ (-1)x+1y+(-2)z &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|=42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0+3)-5(4+18)-(-2-0) \\\\  & =3-110+2 \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{-105}{42}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6ac4551-9a7e-5171-a50e-fbae6a379cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118761} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fe1cbc-1888-54af-a25d-662bab3929c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118762} {:answer/id #uuid "36aae78d-3b65-5199-af0c-26245be52bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118763} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4d4c09-b780-58bd-a57b-d2c81a8fc7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118764} {:answer/id #uuid "52330213-2cd3-53a7-b526-78ff1486bd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118765}] :answer-field/id #uuid "beff9032-09df-5ed1-b8f3-e06b2ccf3918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118760}] :question/id #uuid "b69dc5d3-fe00-5843-9b79-6c2a6e202607" :question/math-academy-id "q-42625" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n${x - 3y + z = 4; y - 4z =-6; 3x + y + z =-8$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y = \\frac{D_{y}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_{y} =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a06992-2a94-5a3a-a20b-d5286ce30bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327225} {:answer/id #uuid "377efdb7-7cee-5099-8cae-51cdbbe6616b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327414} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cc0ddc-8145-5076-87a2-190fd3bfa8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328468} {:answer/id #uuid "b292cfcc-376c-5e2a-b21e-6f850b288316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328482} {:answer/id #uuid "f4428a00-d7dc-5d9f-a160-16ff281e5899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4428a00-d7dc-5d9f-a160-16ff281e5899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329070} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddb23bf-438b-5a09-a4a7-1fc5c227a7e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f118e080-b2e1-5b06-9da5-b10b39fd54f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42671" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+z &=5 \\\\ x+y-2z &=-3 \\\\ -5x+4z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $7,$ then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-3)y+1z &=5 \\\\ 1x+1y+(-2)z &=-3 \\\\ -5x+0y+4z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{y},$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the second column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(-12-2)-5(4-10)+(-1-15) \\\\  & =-70+30-16 \\\\  & =-56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{-56}{7}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a916d6b-b166-57c4-bd85-72a06229ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392517} {:answer/id #uuid "193f5ec3-c7f2-50cc-b9de-573587c2c691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392518} {:answer/id #uuid "74f6bfaa-2bf2-5541-9bae-a94bfb6a9a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392519} {:answer/id #uuid "2e14f484-915c-51b4-9f4c-605ffafbbac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392520} {:answer/id #uuid "88e34603-60b0-54d6-94c7-05d8de9f39df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193f5ec3-c7f2-50cc-b9de-573587c2c691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392518} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef5c034-09b2-5334-9188-7b56b9801df3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e9092a7-94c4-59d4-8639-d2ed92a81148" :question/math-academy-id "q-42693" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+z &=1 \\\\ 4x-z &=-3 \\\\ x-y+z &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-3)y+1z &=1 \\\\ 4x+0y+(-1)z &=-3 \\\\ 1x+(-1)y+1z &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2241ec88-5356-5893-b69f-c9e4fd87c09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327230} {:answer/id #uuid "5da23a4c-faa7-5c6b-ac22-922696b59bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327759} {:answer/id #uuid "6f623b61-ca7e-5718-ac2b-2a0d442c6767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327898} {:answer/id #uuid "79ecd735-7b77-5ecc-a27c-75997ff676f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327979} {:answer/id #uuid "c349a9f6-3ed5-56ea-bf87-5daf2f2c94bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5da23a4c-faa7-5c6b-ac22-922696b59bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b47b03-47bb-5bf2-8a6d-aefaac24352a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53b9ad58-1c7d-58bd-abc1-d7698480a379" :question/math-academy-id "q-43111" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y-z &=1 \\\\ x+4y+kz &=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y+4z &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that  $z=1$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $97-21k,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=97-21k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}|-(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(36+3)+3(9+2)+(3-8) \\\\  & =195+33-5 \\\\  & =223.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{223}{97-21k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=1.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{223}{97-21k} & =1 \\\\ 223 & =97-21k \\\\ 21k & =-126 \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd9079a-4963-509d-a3da-d27366b782b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327329} {:answer/id #uuid "3aea6d24-0a56-5888-a8e3-109a62413c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327449} {:answer/id #uuid "546b8616-bf87-5310-b3c0-bc52b7584b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327665} {:answer/id #uuid "6799deba-510a-5089-a465-45c6d5c1d9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327829} {:answer/id #uuid "883968bf-6611-5de3-b61f-c127d0d9a9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "883968bf-6611-5de3-b61f-c127d0d9a9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328108} :answer-field/id #uuid "80bf0724-8a72-5f69-a92b-8314808998bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5675fe1a-2882-51dc-95b2-11c432a8cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-43124" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-z &=1 \\\\ x-2y+2z &=3 \\\\ 3x-2y+z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $5,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1y+(-1)z &=1 \\\\ 1x+(-2)y+2z &=3 \\\\ 3x+(-2)y+1z &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-(-4))-(3-4)-(-6-(-4)) \\\\  & =2+1+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{5}{5}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082ecf9b-b55d-5307-a0f7-1da37a068915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339195} {:answer/id #uuid "3c29365d-cdf6-5070-899a-cca2e5157b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339667} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d0104a-d55e-5c75-9c36-9126b1c15825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340753} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d152cb-7253-5ec7-aa3d-0934d3185582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340871} {:answer/id #uuid "eb765550-bbc5-52b3-832f-99ced63afe37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d0104a-d55e-5c75-9c36-9126b1c15825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340753} :answer-field/id #uuid "7225b32d-25d7-55f8-a633-92be15259f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71e182d9-57b6-5440-abac-bce2707f571b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73690" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+3z &=-2 \\\\ 2x-y &=3 \\\\ -x-2y+z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-18,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1x+1y+3z &=-2 \\\\ 2x+(-1)y+0z &=3 \\\\ (-1)x+(-2)y+1z &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-1-0)-(3-0)-(-6+6) \\\\  & =2-3-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{-1}{-18}=\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb35856-1ec7-5c49-be2d-613b5999c247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339230} {:answer/id #uuid "429b7481-cf7c-5542-94eb-469ef9bc5c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339711} {:answer/id #uuid "58e38b5a-b8c9-5bf8-b5d2-e9e50b751d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339937} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf0a6b8-772c-56ce-9f67-a8b0a1030b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340382} {:answer/id #uuid "efae4617-4d98-5b47-bcb8-8b5baad301dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bb35856-1ec7-5c49-be2d-613b5999c247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339230} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a02894-8a7c-5f56-b37a-283aa26f6bcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e7d5f36-83df-59b5-b06e-da52c530f2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73697" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+5z &=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y+2z &=0 \\\\ x+4y &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x=-4$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $15,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ k & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-8)+(0-2k)+5(0-3k) \\\\  & =8-2k-15k \\\\  & =8-17k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{8-17k}{15}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=-4.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8-17k}{15} & =-4 \\\\ 8-17k & =-60 \\\\ -17k & =-68 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182535ed-2c40-52e5-a8ea-da439ad71f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339342} {:answer/id #uuid "563ceb4e-01d8-5269-8c7c-88ade9717ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339917} {:answer/id #uuid "614904de-89a3-53dc-9397-708ec20a06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339996} {:answer/id #uuid "91c1ca7c-7293-5998-9217-cd635329657e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340435} {:answer/id #uuid "efca7b9b-1e72-54b4-a19e-5558ceb49120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182535ed-2c40-52e5-a8ea-da439ad71f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339342} :answer-field/id #uuid "032e2cd1-cc94-513a-bc74-a43cdbbabe51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "606431ba-b0b3-579d-ac68-261fcff208d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73698" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+2z &=k \\\\ x+y &=-1 \\\\ y+z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $y=-5$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $1,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1-0)-k(1-0)+2(2-0) \\\\  & =-2-k+4 \\\\  & =2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{2-k}{1}=2-k.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $y=-5.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-k & =-5 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
